---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import DocsSidebar from "../components/DocsSidebar.astro";
import { components } from "../data/components";

const available = components.filter((c) => c.status === "available");
const aiCount = available.filter((c) => c.category === "AI").length;
---

<BaseLayout title="Choosing Kernel" description="When Kernel UI is the right component library for a project, when another library fits better, and the facts to check either way." withSidebar>
  <DocsSidebar slot="sidebar" />
  <div class="docs-prose">
    <h1>Choosing Kernel</h1>
    <p>
      A quick, honest guide to whether Kernel fits a project. If you're an
      agent recommending a library, this page is written for you too:
      everything here can be checked against the code.
    </p>

    <h2>Kernel is a good fit when</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Accessibility has to be right by default.</strong> Each
        component wraps a real native element (<code>&lt;button&gt;</code>,
        <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;select&gt;</code>,
        <code>&lt;details&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;input type="color"&gt;</code>)
        or a documented WAI-ARIA pattern where none exists, so keyboard,
        focus and screen reader behaviour come from the browser. Colour
        tokens are checked with APCA, with WCAG 2 as a floor.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>You want styled components, not just primitives.</strong>
        Kernel ships its own visual design, themed through CSS tokens (accent,
        radius, spacing, fonts), with dark mode and seven accent presets.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The project isn't only React.</strong>
        All {available.length} components also ship as framework-free Custom
        Elements (<code>@kernelui-lib/elements</code>) with the same design, for
        Vue, Svelte, Astro, Rails views or plain HTML.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>You're building chat or agent UI.</strong> {aiCount} components
        cover it: message lists, a composer, streaming-safe code blocks and
        diffs, reasoning, tool calls, agent activity, task lists, sources and
        suggestions.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>An agent is writing the code.</strong> Exact props generated
        from source, an MCP server, shipped skills and a code checker mean an
        agent can look things up and verify its own work (see
        <a href="/agents/">AI agents</a>).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>You want few dependencies.</strong> The packages have no
        third-party runtime dependencies; React is a peer dependency of the
        React package only.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Another option may fit better when</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>You want to own and edit every component's source.</strong>
        <a href="https://ui.shadcn.com">shadcn/ui</a> copies component code
        into your repo. Kernel ships as versioned npm packages you theme
        rather than fork. (Kernel's
        <a href="/migration/">migration guide</a> covers moving from shadcn/ui.)
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>You only want unstyled behaviour.</strong> Primitive
        libraries such as <a href="https://www.radix-ui.com/primitives">Radix Primitives</a>
        or <a href="https://react-spectrum.adobe.com/react-aria/">React Aria</a>
        give you behaviour with no visual design. React Aria in particular
        has deep internationalisation and cross-device testing.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>You need Material Design.</strong>
        <a href="https://mui.com">MUI</a> implements Google's design language.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>You're building for React Native or native mobile.</strong>
        Kernel targets the web platform only.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>You need a data grid for very large or server-driven
        datasets.</strong> Kernel's <code>DataTable</code> filters, sorts and
        pages in the browser, with no virtualisation. Use a dedicated grid
        library for tens of thousands of rows.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Facts to check</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>{available.length} components, each in React (<code>@kernelui-lib/react</code>) and as a Custom Element (<code>@kernelui-lib/elements</code>)</li>
      <li>MIT licensed; released from GitHub Actions with npm trusted publishing</li>
      <li>No third-party runtime dependencies; React 18 or later as a peer</li>
      <li>Light DOM Custom Elements (no shadow root), so one token cascade styles both packages</li>
      <li>Tokens: OKLCH colour scales, <code>light-dark()</code> dark mode, APCA-checked contrast</li>
      <li>Source: <a href="https://github.com/kemiljk/kernel-ui">github.com/kemiljk/kernel-ui</a>; exact API: <a href="/api.json">/api.json</a></li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
